===Adverb===
{{en-adv}}

# In an [[abstracted]] manner; [[separately]]; in the [[abstract]]. {{defdate|From early 17th c.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=10}} [This source gives the mid-17th century as the earliest attestation, but compare quotations above.]</ref>
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1610|author=w:Edmund Bolton|title=The Elements of Armories|location=London|publisher=George Eld|chapter=15|page=89|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A16308.0001.001|passage=Doubtlesse, in the ''Idaea'', or mentall shape before it come as it were into act, by beeing painted, cut, or carued, those terminating, and truly ''Mathematical'' lines, '''abstractedly''' considered, are manifest, adhering (or inhering rather) without any possibility of separation from the conceaued Image.}}
#* {{RQ:Barrow Lord's Prayer|subtitle=Honour Thy Father, and Thy Mother|page=164|passage=[I]t is abundant ſatisfaction to them if they ſee their children do vvell; their chief delight and contentment is in their childrens good abſolutely and '''abſtractedly''', vvithout indirect regards to their ovvn advantage.}}
#* {{RQ:Berkeley Analyst|section=L|sectionname=Occasion of this Address. Conclusion. Queries.|page=77|passage=''Qu''[''estion''] 8. VVhether the Notions of abſolute Time, abſolute Place, and abſolute Motion be not moſt '''abſtractedly''' Metaphyſical? VVhether it be poſſible for us to meaſure, compute, or knovv them?}}
#* {{RQ:Boswell Johnson|quotee=Johnson|year=1769|page=311|passage=You remember the gentleman in “The Spectator,” who had a commission of lunacy taken out against him for his extreme singularity, such as never wearing a wig, but a night-cap. Now, Sir, '''abstractedly''', the night-cap was best; but, relatively, the advantage was overbalanced by his making the boys run after him.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1852|author=w:John Pollard Seddon|title=Progress in Art and Architecture|location=London|publisher=David Bogue|chapter=3|page=38|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/010363408|passage={{...}} as the head of a beast is to be placed only upon its shoulders, so neither is a plant, however '''abstractedly''' treated, to be placed root uppermost {{...}}}}
# With [[absence of mind]] {{defdate|From the early 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1806|author=w:Isaac D'Israeli|title=Flim-Flams!|location=London|publisher=[[w:John Murray (publishing house)|John Murray]]|edition=2nd|volume=2|chapter=38|page=219|url=https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/008394538|passage=Leaning '''abstractedly''' over a hogshead of tallow, her dark dishevelled tresses waved in opposite directions, and a Muse (as she was) appeared to vulgar eyes, a Fury!}}
#* {{RQ:Isaac Taylor Ultimate Civilization|section=I|page=257|passage=He is a man of the meditative claſs:—he walks the ſtreets '''abſtractedly''':—as he goes he digeſts enterpriſes, fraught with world-wide benefits.}}
#* {{RQ:Hardy Far from the Madding Crowd|chapter=XII|passage=The farmer had never turned his head once, but with eyes fixed on the most advanced point along the road, passed as unconsciously and '''abstractedly''' as if Bathsheba and her charms were thin air.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=w:Alan Sullivan|title={{w|The Great Divide (novel)|The Great Divide}}|location=London|publisher=Lovat Dickson & Thompson|chapter=10|url=https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20161202/html.php|passage=In Montreal, George Stephen was also thinking about money while he walked in sober mood from the Bank to the Canadian Pacific offices; one of the best-known figures in the city {{...}} his passage along St. James Street was noted by many, and he nodded '''abstractedly''' to innumerable acquaintances.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=w:Alan Hollinghurst|title=[[w:The Stranger's Child|The Stranger’s Child]]|location=London|publisher=Picador|section=section 3, 3|passage=She looked up over the page, ran her eye '''abstractedly''' across the room, but gave no sign at all of seeing him.}}

====Usage notes====
The development of the sense ''with absence of mind'' is illustrated by the following quotation, in which to ''think abstractedly'' is to give one’s full attention to one thing, to the exclusion of all else:
* {{RQ:Edgeworth Fashionable Life|volume=I|chapter=XVI|page=328|passage={{...}} I, this morning as I lay awake in my bed, thought so '''abstractedly''' and attentively, that I heard neither wheels nor hostlers.}}

